1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,625
Part three.

2
00:00:01,038 --> 00:00:02,388
Is AI really creative?

3
00:00:02,812 --> 00:00:04,675
Or is it just remixing what humans already

4
00:00:04,688 --> 00:00:05,038
wrote?

5
00:00:05,462 --> 00:00:06,637
The guests use a balloon.

6
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:10,200
Everything mathematicians have discovered is the inside of

7
00:00:10,212 --> 00:00:10,762
the balloon.

8
00:00:11,175 --> 00:00:13,800
If AI only combines what's already inside,

9
00:00:14,088 --> 00:00:15,688
critics call it the convex hull.

10
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:16,850
Nothing really new.

11
00:00:17,262 --> 00:00:18,788
Alex prefers another picture,

12
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:19,975
from Tim Gowers:

13
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:20,750
tiles.

14
00:00:21,175 --> 00:00:23,200
You keep placing known tiles next to each

15
00:00:23,212 --> 00:00:23,487
other,

16
00:00:23,875 --> 00:00:25,825
and nobody knows how far that can reach.

17
00:00:26,312 --> 00:00:27,463
For quasi R H,

18
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:30,025
every single move was known to humans.

19
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:32,438
Some mathematicians are kicking themselves.

20
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:34,762
But the chain of moves was so long

21
00:00:34,888 --> 00:00:37,213
and so beautiful that Alex calls it shocking.

22
00:00:37,625 --> 00:00:39,137
Ankur adds something unsettling.

23
00:00:39,562 --> 00:00:41,475
These proofs just don't look like a human

24
00:00:41,488 --> 00:00:41,988
solved them.

25
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,825
It reminds him of image AI that recognizes

26
00:00:44,838 --> 00:00:47,400
cats using patterns humans can't even see.

27
00:00:47,812 --> 00:00:49,350
Another guest puts it more hopefully.

28
00:00:49,762 --> 00:00:51,150
Even if it's only remixing,

29
00:00:51,438 --> 00:00:52,338
it lifts the fog.

30
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:54,550
It's like giving mathematicians radar.

31
00:00:54,962 --> 00:00:56,200
And it redraws the map.

32
00:00:56,612 --> 00:00:58,887
An Anthropic result on a problem called three

33
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:00,762
sum, the kind of puzzle you get in

34
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:04,088
coding interviews, turned out faster than almost anyone

35
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:05,062
believed possible.

36
00:01:05,475 --> 00:01:07,325
An entire theory had been built on that

37
00:01:07,338 --> 00:01:08,425
problem being hard.

38
00:01:08,838 --> 00:01:11,475
Now that whole field has to reorganize itself.

39
00:01:11,888 --> 00:01:12,450
There's a catch,

40
00:01:12,488 --> 00:01:12,725
though.

41
00:01:13,138 --> 00:01:14,850
The AI didn't win every time.

42
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:16,325
According to Ankur,

43
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,962
thousands of problems were reportedly attempted,

44
00:01:19,338 --> 00:01:21,212
and only the successes were published,

45
00:01:21,562 --> 00:01:22,575
roughly one in ten.

46
00:01:22,988 --> 00:01:25,825
Nobody knows exactly where the line between solved

47
00:01:25,975 --> 00:01:27,212
and unsolved runs.

48
00:01:27,625 --> 00:01:28,613
As one guest puts it,

49
00:01:28,912 --> 00:01:31,400
they're readjusting to their inability to predict.
